 >> [Also, to make this work easily, I'd like to play
 >> with the much nicer Crawl "local and global level
 >> display" interface, while I'm wishing for the
 >> moon.]

 > What does it do?

See:

http://www.well.com/user/xanthian/images/CrawlDoubleLevelMapExample.png

where the left hand view is the bottom right corner
of the right hand view.

There are two views of the level, sitting side by
side.

One view is an incomplete "local" one, on the left
side, with large grid squares, in which the PC
roams, showing the "local fighting scene". In the
example, in tiles mode, that is the part with the
orange bricks for walls. There is nothing to prevent
those tiles being replaced by character shaped
tiles, for those who prefer not to play in a tiles
image type mode.

The another view, black white and gray in the
example, is a complete, "overview" one, with small
grid squares, maybe 2x2, that just show which parts
of the whole level you've discovered so far with
little more detail than that. The stairs in the
overview are shown as colored 2x2 squares, and there
may be other essential items shown on that view (I'm
a newbie at Crawl). The position of the PC on the
overview grid is shown by a tick mark above and to
the side, whose "intersection" points to the PC.

 >> This task is "very difficult" because, while the
 >> dimension sizes are already parameterized in the
 >> game, cripplingly many places that they should be
 >> checked in a calculation, it seems that they are
