 >> pretty much a preen of the entire source code
 >> base.

 > Wouldn't also object creation have to rebalanced?
 > Otherwise you'd get a lot more objects just
 > because there are more rooms that get random
 > objects.

Well, yes, but you'd get them in return for exerting
proportionally more effort. Probably the rebalancing
would be more in terms of a lower proportion of
exceptionally good (or bad) objects, rather than a
change in their number. One thing that would need
careful tuning would be the availability of
foodstuffs other than monster corpses; having to
explore farther just to find the stairs down makes
the chance of starving greater.

Another issue, I expect, is that on big enough
levels, if monster generation is done at a rate "per
grid square", then monsters will arrive from distant
starting points clumped together by the time they
get to the PC, from the funneling effect of the
passages.

Still, I'd rather play on squarish, 1000x1000 levels
than on the tiny ones in current use. That, over
time, would leave space to put many more special
"subset" rooms, like temples and shops and zoos and
hives and newly invented types, into a level and
still have them be quite hard even to locate in all
that space.

While I'm asking for work I have no intention of
doing, changing game-wide the integers used to
express the odds for portioning out objects,
monsters, traps and such stuff to have a couple more
significant digits, so that a finer gradation of
"fraction of weapons that are pole-arms" and such,
could be done, would be helpful to code maintainers
and patch writers.

Even better, or in addition, fixing the ranges over
which random numbers are cast to go from "zero to
N-1", where N-1 is a computed value got by summing
the existing "odds" integers for all possibilities
(at compile time), rather than "zero to fixed value"
set at code architecture time, would make adding new
types of "things" to random choice lists _much_
easier.

